{
  "paper_id": "nar2026-gkag515",
  "title": "Microbe Decoder uncovers functional traits of microbes in microbiome datasets.",
  "doi": "10.1093/nar/gkag515",
  "journal": "Nucleic Acids Research",
  "data_url": "https://github.com/thackmann/microbedecoder (mirrored at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18651323)",
  "verified": {
    "opened_data_files": true,
    "what_i_actually_checked": "Downloaded and opened from GitHub: database_clean.csv (27,010 rows x 55 columns, parsed first try in pandas), database.csv (same 27,010 rows, 97 columns, needs latin-1 encoding, carries pre-split LPSN_Phylum/Class/Order/Family/Genus columns), gene_functions_database.rds (read in R: 22,734,429 genome-to-KEGG-orthology-ID pairs, 15,100 genomes, 11,866 distinct KO IDs, median 1,474 KOs per genome), and 16S_ribosomal_sequences.zip (21,119 FASTA files named by LPSN ID, one sequence each, median length 1,461 bp in a 300-file sample). Verified the joins: 15,100 of 15,101 IMG genome IDs in the table match the KO table; 21,119 of 21,119 FASTA filenames match an LPSN ID row. Counted labels with features attached (oxygen tolerance 10,855; Gram stain 9,977; motility 9,318; spore formation 4,853; fermentation type 8,023; growth temperature 15,356), class balance, 0 duplicate LPSN IDs, 10 duplicate genus/species/strain tuples, 4,522 genera and 39 phyla. Opened the supplement (Supplementary Datasets.xlsx) and recomputed the paper's headline numbers from Dataset S3. Listed the GitHub tree: 30 pre-trained random forest .rds models ship with the repo. Checked the Zenodo record (CC-BY-4.0, software).",
    "what_came_from_paper_text_only": "That BacDive, FAPROTAX and Fermentation Explorer trait values are literature-curated rather than machine-predicted; the exact procedure behind the 70/30 and rank-held-out evaluation splits; the claim of 38 reference metabolic networks (the shipped config file has ~42 rows); Supplementary Figure S5 (the accuracy-versus-taxonomic-rank decay curve), which I could not render because the supplement PDF needs poppler."
  },
  "part_a_killers": {
    "a1_downloadable": {"pass": true, "note": "Fetched every file directly from raw.githubusercontent.com with no login, no access application, no dead links. Zenodo mirror is open, CC-BY-4.0."},
    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": true, "note": "Traits are literal columns in database_clean.csv ('Oxygen tolerance (BacDive)', 'Gram stain (BacDive)', 'Motility (BacDive)', 'Spore formation (BacDive)', 'Type of metabolism (Fermentation Explorer)'). Multi-value cells are semicolon-separated and split with one line of code; no cross-source inference needed."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": true, "note": "Inputs are physically present, not accessions: 21,119 16S ribosomal RNA sequences as FASTA text, and 22.7 million organism-to-KEGG-orthology-ID pairs in the shipped .rds. No external lookups required."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "Categorical traits use a fixed controlled vocabulary (aerobe/anaerobe/facultative anaerobe; positive/negative/variable). The one continuous trait names its unit in the column header: 'Temperature for growth in degrees (BacDive)'. Sequence lengths are base pairs."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": true, "n_rows": 21119, "note": "21,119 organisms carry a 16S sequence; of those, 8,581 have an unambiguous oxygen-tolerance call, 9,868 a Gram stain, 8,947 a motility call, 8,023 a fermentation type. Every target clears the 1,500-row bar."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~0.4 GB", "note": "36 MB label table, 40 MB KO table, ~60 MB of FASTA, plus a cached embedding matrix of 21,119 x 768 floats (~65 MB). Peak memory under 4 GB; no GPU needed for a frozen-backbone baseline."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "Both database exports contain exactly 27,010 rows. The paper reports mean balanced accuracy of 0.81 / 0.86 / 0.88 for its three tools; recomputing from Supplementary Dataset S3 gives 0.811 (147 traits) / 0.855 (38) / 0.875 (30). The paper says 30 pre-trained machine-learning models and the repo ships exactly 30."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": true, "note": "PASS ONLY FOR PART OF THE TABLE. BacDive and Fermentation Explorer columns are curated from published strain descriptions and Bergey's Manual (rule-based text extraction plus a manual label file) - real measurements. The 'Type of metabolism (FAPROTAX)' and '(FAPROTAX2)' columns are NOT: I read getFAPROTAXpredictions.R and they are produced by running the FAPROTAX tool on taxonomy strings, i.e. a rule-based tool propagating labels down the taxonomy. Those two columns must never be the prediction target; using them would be learning a lookup table from phylogeny."
  },
  "part_a_verdict": "pass",
  "score_items": {
    "B1": {"score": 1, "why": "Three files - one 27,010-row label table, one KO table, one FASTA folder - joined on LPSN ID and IMG genome ID; joins verified complete."},
    "B2": {"score": 2, "why": "database_clean.csv parsed first try in pandas; .rds read first try in R; FASTA trivial. Only the raw export needs latin-1."},
    "B3": {"score": 1, "why": "Labels come from three curation sources with different vocabularies; each is internally consistent but cross-source harmonisation is needed."},
    "B4": {"score": 1, "why": "1,870 of 10,874 oxygen-tolerance cells hold conflicting multi-values; 372 motility cells say 'negative;positive'. Droppable, but a policy is required."},
    "B5": {"score": 2, "why": "Zero duplicate LPSN IDs across 27,010 rows; only 10 duplicated genus/species/subspecies/strain tuples. Verified in the file."},
    "B6": {"score": 1, "why": "Column names self-document their source database, and build scripts are public, but there is no data dictionary for the value vocabularies."},
    "C1": {"score": 2, "why": "'How far from training clades can a trait still be called, and from which input?' is informative whichever way it resolves."},
    "C2": {"score": 1, "why": "MICROPHERRET, BacDive-AI, phenotrex and this paper's own 30 models are open, but all take genome gene content, not 16S sequence."},
    "C3": {"score": 2, "why": "Unique-application plus better-evaluation angle: no retrieved work predicts organism physiology directly from a 16S sequence with a DNA language model."},
    "C4": {"score": 2, "why": "The alternative is culturing an isolate and running Gram stain, oxygen-tolerance and spore assays in a lab; most microbes cannot be cultured at all."},
    "C5": {"score": 2, "why": "Supplementary Dataset S3 gives per-trait published balanced accuracies (aerobe 0.94, Gram-negative 0.974, motility 0.821); majority class is the dummy."},
    "D1": {"score": 2, "why": "DNABERT-2 and HyenaDNA have open downloadable weights and handle 1.5 kb 16S sequences; Nucleotide Transformer v2 is a third option."},
    "D2": {"score": 2, "why": "21,119 sequences embedded once on CPU and cached; the head is logistic regression or gradient boosting on a 768-dimension vector."},
    "D3": {"score": 2, "why": "DNABERT-2 and HyenaDNA are Apache-2.0; the paper, its Zenodo record and the BacDive trait data are CC-BY-4.0. Verified the Zenodo licence field."},
    "E1": {"ok": true, "why": "database.csv ships LPSN_Genus/Family/Order/Class/Phylum columns; I saw them, so clade-held-out splits need no extra work."},
    "E2": {"ok": true, "why": "Among oxygen-labelled organisms: 2,597 genera; among genome-matched organisms: 4,522 genera and 39 phyla. Counted in the file."},
    "E3": {"ok": true, "why": "Majority class (aerobe is 52% of unambiguous oxygen calls), per-genus mean, and nearest-16S-neighbour lookup are all computable."},
    "E4": {"ok": true, "why": "Conflicting multi-value cells (1,870 oxygen, 372 motility) give an empirical curator-disagreement rate; overlapping traits across BacDive and Fermentation Explorer cross-check."},
    "E5": {"ok": true, "why": "Gram stain should be near-perfectly recoverable (it tracks phylum), and the 131 methanogens are all Methanobacteriota - textbook signals the model must find."},
    "E6": {"ok": true, "why": "Label shuffling and input-block ablation (16S only, KO only, taxonomy name only) are all cheap on a cached embedding matrix."},
    "E7": {"ok": true, "why": "Full BacDive release (99,392 strains) extends beyond the 27,010 type strains here; the paper's own rumen isolates were measured independently in the authors' lab."},
    "E8": {"ok": true, "why": "The 30 released random forest .rds models plus Supplementary Dataset S3's per-trait numbers mean the paper's own result can be reproduced."},
    "G1": {"score": 2, "why": "Verified in the file: NCBI taxonomy ID (15,584), GTDB ID (15,584), IMG genome ID (15,101), BacDive ID (19,114), GOLD organism ID (15,588)."},
    "G2": {"score": 2, "why": "GTDB (hundreds of thousands of genomes) and BacDive (99,392 strains) both join on identifiers already present in the table."},
    "G3": {"score": 2, "why": "Organism-to-16S is one-to-one (21,119/21,119) and organism-to-genome is one-to-one by construction ('IMG Genome ID max quality', 15,101 unique)."},
    "G4": {"score": 2, "why": "Combined question: can cultured type strains assign physiology to the uncultured majority in GTDB, and at what phylogenetic distance does it break?"},
    "H1": {"score": 2, "why": "Negatives are systematically recorded: 5,334 motility-negative vs 3,613 positive; 3,199 spore-negative vs 1,578 positive. Counted in the file."},
    "H2": {"score": 1, "why": "39 phyla, bacteria and archaea, but heavily skewed (5,639 Pseudomonadota of 15,100) and limited to cultured, validly published type strains."},
    "H3": {"score": 2, "why": "The honest claim is writable in one sentence, and it is about cultured type strains only."},
    "H4": {"score": 2, "why": "The discussion states plainly that the database holds cultured prokaryotes only and that uncultured organisms and eukaryotes are future work."},
    "I1": {"score": 2, "why": "Hackmann lab (UC Davis), the DSMZ BacDive/Koblitz group, the MICROPHERRET group in Padova, and JGI IMG/M (a co-author is there)."},
    "I2": {"score": 2, "why": "A decay curve tells amplicon-based labs which clades a trait call can be trusted for, and which lineages are worth culturing."},
    "I3": {"score": 2, "why": "Per-clade balanced accuracy and calibrated probability are exactly what a user deciding 'do I believe this trait call?' needs; the tools already output probabilities."},
    "I4": {"score": 1, "why": "Nearest-16S-neighbour is a strong baseline for phylogenetically conserved traits and may win; that is the honest risk."},
    "J1": {"score": 2, "why": "Frozen pretrained backbone, grouped cross-validation, calibration and a transfer question - real modelling, not just cleaning."},
    "J2": {"score": 2, "why": "Sanity checks are textbook: Gram stain tracks phylum, methanogens are archaea, obligate aerobes carry cytochrome oxidase genes."},
    "J3": {"score": 2, "why": "A cleaned 21,119-sequence 16S-plus-phenotype benchmark with clade-held-out splits is shareable even if no model beats the baseline."},
    "J4": {"score": 2, "why": "Four weeks: one to build the table, one to cache embeddings, two to evaluate and write up."},
    "J5": {"score": 1, "why": "Kill condition stated here (no gain over nearest-neighbour on held-out families by week two) but no calendar date committed in NOTES.md yet."}
