  "backbone": {
    "name": "DNABERT-2 (a DNA language model, 117M parameters, Apache-2.0) on the 1.5 kb 16S ribosomal RNA sequence; HyenaDNA or Nucleotide Transformer v2 as alternatives",
    "freeze_and_head_viable": true,
    "hardware": "Laptop CPU. Embed 21,119 sequences once, cache a 21,119 x 768 float matrix (~65 MB), then fit a logistic-regression or gradient-boosting head per trait in seconds.",
    "licence": "DNABERT-2 and HyenaDNA are Apache-2.0 (permissive). Nucleotide Transformer checkpoints are partly non-commercial, so prefer the first two. Data is CC-BY-4.0."
  },
  "integration": {
    "partner_dataset": "GTDB (the Genome Taxonomy Database, hundreds of thousands of bacterial and archaeal genomes, most from uncultured lineages) and the full BacDive release (99,392 strains with curated physiology)",
    "join_key": "GTDB ID and NCBI taxonomy ID, both already columns in database_clean.csv for 15,584 organisms; BacDive ID for 19,114",
    "bigger_question_one_sentence": "Can physiology measured on ~26,000 cultured type strains be extended to the uncultured majority of life, and at what phylogenetic distance from a cultured relative does that extension stop being trustworthy?"
  },
  "controls": {
    "positive_control": "Two, chosen in advance: Gram stain must be recovered at high accuracy because it tracks phylum (6,034 negative vs 3,772 positive in the 16S subset), and all 131 methanogens must be called methanogenic - they are all Methanobacteriota. A plumbing check is predicting the 16S sequence's own GC content from its embedding, which must be near-perfect.",
    "negative_control": "Shuffle trait labels within the clade-held-out folds and confirm balanced accuracy collapses to 0.5; replace embeddings with random vectors of the same shape; ablate input blocks (16S sequence only, KEGG orthology only, taxonomy name only) to see which block does the work.",
    "external_validation": "Three levels: (1) the full BacDive release, which covers 99,392 strains versus the 27,010 LPSN type strains here; (2) the authors' own rumen isolates, characterised in their lab for anaerobic growth, aerobic respiration, nitrate respiration, Gram reaction, spore formation and motility (Supplementary Dataset S4), a genuinely independent measurement set though only tens of isolates; (3) the 6,215 organisms that have a 16S sequence but no genome, which the genome-based tools cannot touch at all."
  },
  "honest_claim_h3": "For a bacterium or archaeon whose 16S ribosomal RNA sequence we have, this model predicts laboratory-measured physiology - oxygen tolerance, Gram stain, motility, spore formation, fermentation type - at an accuracy that we quantify as a function of how far it sits from the nearest cultured, validly published type strain in the training set; it says nothing about organisms from lineages with no cultured relative at any rank, and nothing about eukaryotes.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use a microbe's 16S ribosomal RNA gene sequence to predict its laboratory-measured physiology, and measure how that fails for distant relatives.",
    "train_on": "One 16S ribosomal RNA gene sequence per organism (median 1,461 base pairs), for 21,119 named bacteria and archaea - I counted the FASTA files and confirmed every one matches a row in the trait table.",
    "predict": "Five or six physiological traits per organism: oxygen tolerance (8,581 unambiguous labels), Gram stain (9,868), motility (8,947), spore formation (4,777), fermentation type (8,023) and optimal growth temperature in degrees (15,356). The true answers come from BacDive and Bergey's Manual, which record what microbiologists measured when they cultured and described each strain.",
    "why_worthwhile": "16S sequencing is the cheapest and most common way to survey a microbial community, but today it can only yield physiology by looking the organism's name up in a table - which fails for the many organisms that have no close cultured relative. A decay curve showing exactly where that failure begins tells every amplicon-based microbiome lab which of its trait calls to trust."
  },
  "paper_type": "computational",
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "Our labelled set is comparable in size to BacDive-AI's (15,938 genomes) and this paper's own (15,100), not bigger. The genuine edge is modality coverage: 6,215 organisms have a 16S sequence but no genome, so no existing tool - all of which need genome gene content - can make any prediction for them."
    },
    "confidence_score": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "The data supports a real uncertainty estimate: 1,870 of 10,874 oxygen-tolerance cells and 372 motility cells record curators disagreeing, giving an empirical noise floor, and clade-held-out folds give distance-conditioned error bars. But BacDive-AI already publishes a per-prediction confidence score, so this is not an unoccupied gap."
    },
    "models_beatable": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "BacDive-AI (2025) used a random 80/20 split with stratified 10-fold cross-validation - leakage-prone across related strains - so its F1 of 0.95 is almost certainly optimistic and is re-evaluable. But this paper (2026) already ran genus-to-phylum held-out splits on its own tool, so the honest-evaluation gap is half closed already."
    },
    "guaranteed_improvement": {
      "answer": "no",
      "why": "Condition failed: our model does not see everything the baselines see. The 16S-only model sees strictly less than the genome-based random forests, so on organisms that have a genome it should lose. The gain is on the 6,215 genome-less organisms, where the baseline cannot run at all - which is a coverage win, not a head-to-head improvement."
    }
  },
  "label_elsewhere": {
    "score": 2,
    "sources": "BacDive, the DSMZ strain-phenotype database, which the Koblitz et al. 2025 paper I retrieved states covers 99,392 strains of 21,578 species with over 1.2 million curated datapoints. Also known (not searched this session): the Madin et al. condensed bacterial and archaeal trait database in Scientific Data, and GTDB for the taxonomy backbone.",
    "approx_items": 99392,
    "join_key": "BacDive ID (present for 19,114 of 27,010 rows, verified), NCBI taxonomy ID (15,584), or genus-species name",
    "why": "The same traits are measured for roughly four times as many strains outside this table, so a model trained here has a large ready-made external test set and could be retrained at larger scale if the approach works."
  },
  "leakage_plan": {
    "risks": [
      "Close relatives on both sides of the split: the table holds 10,855 oxygen-labelled organisms across only 2,597 genera, so a random split puts congeners in train and test and inflates every number - exactly the failure the 2025 Bioinformatics review names as the field's main open problem.",
      "Taxonomy-propagated labels masquerading as measurements: the 'Type of metabolism (FAPROTAX)' and '(FAPROTAX2)' columns are output from running the FAPROTAX tool on taxonomy strings (I read the script), so training on them means learning phylogeny from phylogeny.",
      "Machine-predicted values leaking in from BacDive itself: BacDive now hosts 50,396 machine-learning predictions alongside curated values, so a newer export could contain another model's output as our label.",
      "The 16S sequence is itself the basis of the taxonomy used to curate some trait assignments, so sequence and label are not fully independent for literature-propagated entries.",
      "Phylum imbalance: 5,639 of 15,100 genome-matched organisms are Pseudomonadota, so a model can score well by learning two or three big clades."
    ],
    "split": "Nested clade-held-out cross-validation on the LPSN_Genus, LPSN_Family, LPSN_Order, LPSN_Class and LPSN_Phylum columns - I saw all five as real columns in database.csv, so no taxonomy parsing is needed. Report one accuracy per held-out rank, which is the headline result. Add a 16S sequence-identity split (cluster at 97% and 90% identity with MMseqs2 or VSEARCH) as an independent check that does not rely on taxonomic labels.",
    "checks": [
      "Shuffle labels within each held-out clade fold; balanced accuracy must fall to 0.5.",
      "For every test organism, record the 16S identity to its nearest training sequence, and plot accuracy against that identity - the curve, not a single number, is the deliverable.",
      "Train on the nearest-neighbour feature alone (predict the trait of the most similar training sequence) and report it as the baseline the model must beat.",
      "Never use the FAPROTAX or FAPROTAX2 columns as a target; use them only as a comparison method.",
      "Confirm the BacDive export used (advsearch_bacdive_2024-11-07.csv, dated before BacDive-AI's predictions were deposited) carries no predicted-value flag."
    ],
    "verdict": "Manageable. The split columns already exist in the file and the one label-provenance trap is identified and avoidable by dropping two named columns."
  },
  "success_criteria": {
    "metric": "Balanced accuracy per trait at each held-out taxonomic rank (genus, family, order, class, phylum), because the decision a user makes is 'do I trust this trait call for an organism this far from anything cultured?', and because balanced accuracy is what the paper reports, so the comparison is direct.",
    "baselines": "Three. (1) Majority class - aerobe is 52% of unambiguous oxygen calls, Gram-negative 61%, motility-negative 60%; counted in the file. (2) Nearest-16S-neighbour label transfer, the honest dummy for a phylogenetically conserved trait. (3) The paper's own published per-trait numbers from Supplementary Dataset S3: aerobe 0.94, anaerobe 0.858, Gram-negative 0.974, Gram-positive 0.973, motility 0.821, fermentation 0.896 - all at a split where species can be shared, so they are an upper bound rather than a fair target.",
    "success": "A win is: the frozen DNA-language-model head beats nearest-16S-neighbour by at least 0.05 balanced accuracy on held-out FAMILIES for at least three of the five categorical traits, and produces the first published accuracy-versus-phylogenetic-distance curve for 16S-only trait prediction covering all 6,215 organisms that have no genome.",
    "noise_ceiling": null,
    "kill": "Stop if, after one week of modelling, the model is within 0.02 balanced accuracy of nearest-16S-neighbour at every held-out rank for every trait - that means the sequence model has learned nothing beyond similarity, and the honest output is a one-page note rather than a project. Also stop immediately if the BacDive export turns out to contain machine-predicted values."
  },
  "publication_target": {
    "ambitious": {
      "journal": "Genome Biology",
      "why": "Publishes method papers that change how a common assay is analysed; it would take the 16S-only model clearly beating both nearest-neighbour and taxonomy lookup at family-and-above distance, with the gain demonstrated on organisms that have no sequenced genome."
    },
    "realistic": {
      "journal": "Bioinformatics",
      "why": "The likely result is a careful cross-tool benchmark with a phylogenetic-distance decay curve and a modest, honest gain over nearest-neighbour - exactly the applications-note-plus-benchmark format Bioinformatics runs."
    },
    "fallback": {
      "journal": "NAR Genomics and Bioinformatics",
      "why": "Even the negative outcome - that a DNA language model adds nothing over 16S sequence identity, and that random-split numbers like BacDive-AI's 0.95 F1 drop sharply under clade-held-out evaluation - is a publishable re-evaluation there, with the cleaned 21,119-sequence benchmark as the deposited artefact."
    }
  },
  "pitch": {
    "headline": "21,119 microbes with a 16S sequence and lab-measured physiology in one joinable table, and 6,215 of them have no genome at all.",
    "what_makes_the_data_unusual": "Every existing trait predictor - MICROPHERRET, BacDive-AI, phenotrex, this paper's own 30 models - needs genome gene content. This table pairs curated physiology with a raw 1.5 kb 16S sequence, the input most microbiome surveys actually produce. CC-BY-4.0, all verified by opening the files.",
    "the_win": "A published accuracy-versus-phylogenetic-distance curve for 16S-only trait calling, plus beating nearest-16S-neighbour by 0.05 balanced accuracy on held-out families against the paper's 0.94 aerobe and 0.974 Gram-negative species-level numbers.",
    "even_if_it_fails": "We learn that 16S trait prediction is nothing more than nearest-neighbour lookup, which is itself worth publishing because BacDive-AI's 0.95 F1 came from a random 80/20 split that never tested this, and we leave behind a clade-split benchmark anyone can reuse.",
    "who_wants_it": "The Hackmann lab at UC Davis, the DSMZ BacDive team, and every amplicon-sequencing microbiome group deciding whether to trust a FAPROTAX trait call for an organism with no cultured relative."
  },
  "ml_idea": "Build a same-organisms, same-splits benchmark of microbial trait prediction across three input modalities, using the 27,010-row Microbe Decoder database. For each of six laboratory-measured traits (oxygen tolerance, Gram stain, motility, spore formation, fermentation type, optimal growth temperature), compare: (a) taxonomy-name lookup, the FAPROTAX approach; (b) KEGG-orthology presence/absence into a random forest, which reproduces this paper's and MICROPHERRET's approach and whose 30 trained models ship in the repo; and (c) the new piece - a frozen DNA language model (DNABERT-2 or HyenaDNA, Apache-2.0) embedding of the organism's 1.5 kb 16S ribosomal RNA sequence with a small classification head. Evaluate every method under nested clade-held-out cross-validation using the LPSN_Genus through LPSN_Phylum columns already in the file, and report accuracy as a function of phylogenetic distance from the training set rather than as a single number. The FAPROTAX and FAPROTAX2 columns are excluded as targets because they are tool output, not measurements. The headline deliverable is the decay curve plus coverage: method (c) is the only one that works for the 6,215 organisms with a 16S sequence and no sequenced genome.",
  "effort": {
    "hours_to_feature_table": 6,
    "working_set_gb": 0.4,
    "needs_gpu": false,
    "peak_ram_gb": 4,
    "embedding_hours": 6,
    "cv_run_minutes": 20,
    "compute_note": "Estimated, not timed. The data sizes and joins are measured: I downloaded and parsed all four files and confirmed every join. Embedding time assumes ~1 second per 1.5 kb sequence for a ~100M-parameter DNA language model on an Apple Silicon CPU, over 21,119 sequences; this is the one number that could be off by a factor of three, and it is a one-time cost because embeddings are cached. The cross-validation estimate is for a linear or gradient-boosting head on a cached 21,119 x 768 matrix, six traits by five held-out ranks.",
    "cheapest_killing_check": "Confirm the BacDive trait values in database_clean.csv are curated measurements and not BacDive's own machine-learning predictions. The export is dated 2024-11-07, before BacDive-AI's 50,396 predictions were deposited, and the oxygen_tolerance.csv header shows no predicted-value flag - but spot-check 20 organisms against the live BacDive record. If the labels are predictions, the whole project is distillation and dies on A8. Second cheapest: compute nearest-16S-neighbour accuracy at each held-out rank before building any model; if it is already near the paper's numbers, the modelling adds nothing."
  },
  "verdict": "do_it",
  "verdict_reason": "Every Part A killer was checked by opening the files, not by reading the paper: 27,010 organisms in one clean table that parses first try, 21,119 16S sequences that join one-to-one on LPSN ID, 22.7 million genome-to-KEGG-orthology pairs, thousands of labels per trait, zero duplicate primary keys, and the split columns for clade-held-out evaluation already present as LPSN_Genus through LPSN_Phylum. I recomputed the paper's own headline accuracies from its supplement and they matched (0.811/0.855/0.875 versus the stated 0.81/0.86/0.88), which is the strongest A7 evidence available. The one real trap - that the FAPROTAX columns are a rule-based tool's taxonomy-propagated output rather than measurements - was found by reading the build script and is avoided by dropping two named columns; the BacDive and Fermentation Explorer labels behind them are genuine literature-curated phenotypes. The field is crowded with open models (MICROPHERRET, BacDive-AI, phenotrex, this paper's own 30 shipped random forests), but all of them require genome gene content, and 6,215 organisms here have a 16S sequence and no genome, which no existing tool can touch. The question - how far from a cultured relative can physiology still be called, and from which input - cannot produce a useless answer, a permissively licensed DNA language model fits the input exactly, and the whole thing runs on a laptop CPU in 0.4 GB."
}
